{
  "dom_id": "step-q115653",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/28a16cda2f97557c23908fcd45928da5d4e11102343c33eaf07d1990fb0b6baf.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=0$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=0,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/ea6ac357860556acba25d5510ff0e65a4b66acc65e035cd218715b0e96807519.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-\\infty ,0)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(0,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=0.$\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/2aafc298a8a7b0856d339582bb1236e9ae97c728eba103c703998e14455fdde5.png)\n\n \n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/04ec8de5abfecc23f3b4fb844b4e725e2a4f94aeb12bef61cda6356a6522826c.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$",
